{% extends "layouts/docs" %}

{% block content %}
	<p>Kiln works with ordinary JavaScript values. Your data goes into a template as it is, with no conversion.</p>

	<h2>Types</h2>

	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Kiln</th><th>JavaScript</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>null</td><td><code>null</code> or <code>undefined</code>. Kiln treats them the same</td></tr>
			<tr><td>boolean</td><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td>number</td><td>A <code>number</code>. There is no separate integer type</td></tr>
			<tr><td>string</td><td>A <code>string</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td>array</td><td>An <code>Array</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td>hash</td><td>A plain object, or a <code>Map</code> with string keys</td></tr>
			<tr><td>set</td><td>A <code>Set</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td>date</td><td>A <code>Date</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td>markup</td><td>A string marked safe to output. See <a href="/docs/language/escaping/">Escaping</a></td></tr>
			<tr><td>object</td><td>A class instance that opts in. See <a href="/docs/api/safe-markup/">Safe markup and classes</a></td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<h2>True and false</h2>

	<p>Conditions such as <code>if</code> treat these values as false: <code>null</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>0</code>, <code>NaN</code>, <code>""</code>, an empty array, an empty hash, and an empty set or map. Everything else is true, including <code>"0"</code> and <code>" "</code>.</p>

	{% apply example(context: { tags: [], title: "0" }) %}
		{% verbatim %}
{% if tags %}Has tags{% else %}No tags{% endif %}
{% if title %}Has a title{% endif %}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<h2>Printing values</h2>

	<p>When you output a value, Kiln turns it into a string:</p>

	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>null</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
			<tr><td>boolean</td><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td>number</td><td>The number, as JavaScript writes it</td></tr>
			<tr><td>string</td><td>The string, escaped</td></tr>
			<tr><td>date</td><td>An ISO 8601 date, such as <code>2026-10-15T09:30:00.000Z</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td>array, hash, set</td><td>An error. Use <code>join</code>, <code>json</code> or a loop</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<p>Printing an array is an error rather than a silent "[object Object]", so mistakes show up straight away. Use <a href="/docs/filters/strings/#json"><code>json</code></a> to print data for debugging.</p>

	<h2>Empty values</h2>

	<p>Some features check whether a value is empty rather than false. A value is empty if it is null, <code>false</code>, <code>""</code>, an empty array, an empty hash, or an empty set or map. Unlike false values, <code>0</code> is not empty.</p>

	{% apply example(context: { stock: 0, name: "" }) %}
		{% verbatim %}
{{ stock|default("Unknown") }}
{{ name|default("Anonymous") }}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>The <a href="/docs/filters/lists/#default"><code>default</code></a> filter and the <a href="/docs/tests/#empty"><code>empty</code></a> test use this rule.</p>
{% endblock %}
